    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate leaning heavily on aggressive base-running and favorable platoon matchups. **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (68.37) leads our lineup today; his matchup against Cavalli is our most efficient play, bolstered by a 14% boost from a dynamic platoon advantage and a high-leverage leadoff spot. Close behind is **Caleb Durbin** (66.18), who benefits significantly from a soft matchup against Scherzer. The 5.10 xERA of the opposing starter, combined with his slow delivery, positions **Caleb Durbin** as a primary target for stolen base production, a critical priority given our focus on the scarcest category in this 5x5 format. Similarly, **Christian Yelich** (65.44) provides strong value despite facing Roki Sasaki; his 13% platoon efficiency gain makes him a high-floor start in the outfield.\n\nOur most complex decision centered on the middle infield and utility slots, particularly regarding **Trea Turner** (64.06) and **Kyle Schwarber** (60.67). Both players face Bradley, and while their efficiency remains in the \"Strong\" category, we are tracking a 44% rain risk in that stadium that could lead to an abbreviated contest or delay. **Dillon Dingler** (55.35) remains a consistent start, as his 1.86s pop time and high xwOBA against Messick outweigh the minor defensive penalties associated with his catcher position. While **Colt Keith** was considered for the bench utility spot, his unfavorable L/L platoon split and the current depth of our lineup necessitated keeping him on the sidelines today. As for our core stars, **Yordan Alvarez**, **Junior Caminero**, and **Carson Benge** are unavailable due to team off-days, while depth pieces like **Lars Nootbaar**, **Nelson Velazquez**, **Casey Schmitt**, **Josh Smith**, **Hector Rodriguez**, **Jacob Gonzalez**, **Luis Pena**, and **Pedro Ramirez** remain ready should **Gary Sanchez** or **Kazuma Okamoto** see their situational metrics dip further against their respective Roki Sasaki and Tolle matchups.",
